From 7db28ebce4bd64e4087bfdc817e4bcf6d79dba8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Lane A <lane-a@local.invalid>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 16:13:59 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 14/18] feat(live): rolling live recorder and cut-from-the-past
 buffer (LIVE_CLIPPING, off)

Live AI clipping MVP, step 1 (approved by Luffy 2026-11-18; architecture in
handoff/LIVE_CLIPPING_FEASIBILITY.md). Not wired into any route or worker yet.

- live/recorder.py: one persistent pull per stream. Page URLs (Twitch,
  YouTube) are resolved with `yt-dlp -g` on every (re)connect (live URLs
  expire; YouTube serves separate video + audio HLS) and ffmpeg reads the
  playlists directly with reconnects; other inputs go straight to ffmpeg.
  Stream copy into N-second segments (no re-encode), one run directory per
  connection with its own wall-clock origin; watchdog with backoff; prunes
  the buffer to keep_seconds.
- live/buffer.py: wall-clock segment index across runs, gap detection (an
  outage is reported, never stitched), pruning, and cut_window(start, end):
  stream-copies the covering segments and returns the exact offset/duration
  of the requested span inside the file, plus gaps and whether the buffer
  could not cover the request.

Evidence: tests play a generated video in real time as a fake live source:
16 s buffered while still recording, a window cut from 10-2 s BEFORE the
detection time, a killed pull reconnects as a new run with the gap reported.
Real stream (NASA's public-domain live feed, 40 s): 7 segments, 35 s
buffered, 0 gaps, a 15 s window cut from 20-5 s before the request, 720p
H.264 + AAC (handoff/live-mvp/real_live_cut_window.mp4).
Tests: test_live_buffer 6/6 (40 s, real-time).

